# deploy/broker.env.example — the broker's configuration and secrets.
#
# Copy to `broker.env` beside the live compose file (NOT into this repo) and
# fill in the two secrets. The compose file is committed; this is the pattern
# `webui.env` and `terminal.env` already use for their services.
#
# `broker.env` is deliberately not committed and `broker.env.example` is:
# Guiding Light §5.1's rule about `.env` versus `.env.example`, applied here.
#
# The compose service sets the rest (APP_ENV, WW_DOCKER_HOST, WW_IMAGE,
# WW_MAX_CONCURRENCY, WW_TTL_GRACE, WW_JANITOR_INTERVAL) in `environment:`,
# where they are the same for every deployment of this stack. What follows is
# only what differs per host — the secrets, plus anything a host wants to
# override. Anything set in both places: compose's `environment:` wins.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# An opaque secret Symfony uses for signing. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# The broker refuses to start in prod without it — the entrypoint's comment
# says why: a broker that starts with a guessed credential is worse than one
# that refuses to start.
APP_SECRET=

# The shared secret every caller presents as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
# Any long random string: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Unset, the bearer gate refuses every /v1 request with 503 rather than
# allowing anonymous callers — because "only the broker holds the Docker
# credential" is false the moment anyone can reach /v1 without a token. The
# MCP endpoint lives under /v1 for exactly this reason.
#
# The client half of this value goes in the *client's* env file too
# (terminal.env for the terminal); the two must match or every call is a 401.
WW_TOKEN=

# ── Optional overrides ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not secrets, and the compose service already sets sane values. Set one here
# only to change it for this host; compose's `environment:` would win, so a
# change meant to take effect belongs in compose.yaml, not here. Listed because
# a host that needs a different value should not have to read the compose file
# to discover the name.

# The image command containers are created from. The compose service pins
# `:develop-base` because that is the tag a push to `main` publishes — the
# release tags (`:latest-base`, `:<version>-base`) do not exist until the first
# `v*.*.*` tag is pushed, and pointing at one before then fails every command
# with "image not found". See docker-bake.hcl.
# WW_IMAGE=digitaladapt/work-warp:develop-base

# How many commands may run at once in one workspace. The unit is the
# workspace, not the session (DOCKER-ACCESS.md §6b).
# WW_MAX_CONCURRENCY=4

# Seconds added to a command's own timeout to produce its `ww.ttl` label — the
# backstop the janitor sweeps on when the broker did not get to it.
# WW_TTL_GRACE=300

# How often the janitor sweeps, in seconds. Read by docker/entrypoint.sh (not
# by PHP), which runs `ww:janitor --every` as a child of the broker process
# whenever WW_DOCKER_HOST is set: no cron, no second service (§6a).
# WW_JANITOR_INTERVAL=60
